Expense Sharing Backend:

-> About the Project
    This project is a backend system for an expense sharing application (similar to Splitwise).
    It allows users to create groups, add shared expenses, and see who owes money and who should receive money.

    The main goal of this assignment was to focus on backend design, API structure, and business logic, so no frontend is implemented.

-> Tech Stack Used
   Java
   Spring Boot
   Spring Data JPA
   PostgreSQL (for persistent storage)
   Docker
   Deployed on Render

-> What This Backend Can Do
   Create users
   Create groups and add users to groups
   Add shared expenses within a group
   Support different expense split types
   Equal split
   Exact amount split
   Percentage split
   Calculate balances for each user in a group

-> How the System Works (High Level)
    1.Users are created first.
    2.A group is created (example: Trip, Roommates, etc.).
    3.Users are added to a group.
    4.Expenses are added inside a group.
    5.Each expense can be split in:
       - Equal split
       - Exact amount split
       - Percentage split
    6.The system calculates balances per group based on all expenses.
    Positive balance → user should receive money
    Negative balance → user owes money

-> API Overview:
   1)Users
    - Create User
    POST /users  Example: POST → https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/users
    Request Body:
    {
      "name": "Raghu Ram",
      "email": "raghuramroyya@gmail.com"
    }
--------------------------------------------------
   - Get All Users
    GET /users
    Example: GET → https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/users

   2)Groups
    - Create Group
    POST /groups
    Example: POST → https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/groups?name=Trip
--------------------------------------------------
    - Add User to Group
    POST /groups/{groupId}/addUser/{userId}
    Example: POST → https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/groups/1/addUser/104
---------------------------------------------------
    - Get Group Balances
    GET /groups/{groupId}/balances
    Example: GET → https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/groups/1/balances

    Response Example:

    {
      "Raghu Ram": 450.0,
      "Nithin": -450.0,
      "Angadi Kanishka": 0.0
    }

    Positive value → user should receive money
    Negative value → user owes money
------------------------------------------------
    - Get Group Settlements
    GET /groups/{groupId}/settlements
    Example: GET → https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/groups/1/settlements

    Response Example:

    [
        {
            "from": "Angadi Kanishka",
            "to": "Raghu Ram",
            "amount": 400.0
        },
        {
            "from": "Angadi Kanishka",
            "to": "Challa Nithin",
            "amount": 250.0
        }
    ]

   3) Expenses
    - Add Expense (Equal Split)
    POST /expenses
    Example: POST → https://expense-sharing-backend-uu00.onrender.com/expenses
    Request Body:

    {
      "description": "Hotel",
      "amount": 900,
      "paidByUserId": 1,
      "groupId": 1,
      "splitType": "EQUAL",
      "participantUserIds": [1, 2, 3]
    }
--------------------------------------------------
    - Add Expense (Exact Amount Split)
    POST /expenses

    {
      "description": "Dinner",
      "amount": 600,
      "paidByUserId": 2,
      "groupId": 1,
      "splitType": "EXACT",
      "exactAmounts": {
        "1": 200,
        "2": 250,
        "3": 150
      }
    }
-------------------------------------------------

    - Add Expense (Percentage Split)

    POST /expenses

    {
      "description": "Cab",
      "amount": 500,
      "paidByUserId": 1,
      "groupId": 1,
      "splitType": "PERCENTAGE",
      "percentages": {
        "1": 50,
        "2": 30,
        "3": 20
      }
    }

  Note: All APIs can be tested using Postman or any REST client.

-> Deployment
   The application is containerized using Docker and deployed on Render.
   PostgreSQL is used as the database to make sure data is not lost when the application restarts.
   Database credentials are managed using environment variables.
